      <trans-unit id="help.common.periods">
        <source>help.common.periods</source>
        <target>Each row is a different period:

• Today, since midnight.
• Yesterday, the whole day.
• The last 7, 14 and 30 days, counted from midnight.

All periods end at the time of your latest odometer reading, which can be several hours old.

Until a reading has been made today, the first row says “No reading yet today”.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.today">
        <source>help.common.today</source>
        <target>Polaris spreads the distance driven between two readings according to your usual driving hours (see “Driving hours” in the lease terms).

If you do not set these times, the distance is spread evenly over the 24 hours of the day, night included. For example, a reading at 21:00 can then attribute part of the kilometres driven during the night to the previous day.

By default the “Today” row is compared with what was expected up to your latest reading. A setting in “Driving hours” lets you compare it with the whole day instead.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.live">
        <source>help.common.live</source>
        <target>Today’s data is not permanently saved yet: Polaris saves it every day at midnight.

Until then, the “Today” row is compared with an estimate refreshed in real time. It shows the values Polaris would save if the midnight job ran at this moment.

This estimate is temporary and is not kept in the history.</target>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.common.fallback">
        <source>help.common.fallback</source>
        <target>If no reference data is available, Polaris uses the latest known record for the comparison. The period is then labelled “Since yesterday” or “Since N days ago”, depending on how old that record is.</target>
      </trans-unit>
